@@ -480,7 +480,7 @@ Display is fixed bank-0 hires (no relocation — MidiScene uploads no SID payloa
 **The wire bounds live here, at the decode boundary.** A `0x30` recipe is a SID *write order*, so it is capped at `MAX_TIMING_RECIPE_PAIRS` (28, the register-table length) and a register id repeated in the order keeps only its first position. Both bounds are load-bearing and neither substitutes for the other: SysEx has no length limit on a virtual/network MIDI port and the recipe persists until the next `0x30`, so an uncapped decode let a 400 KB message turn every subsequent four-register frame into 200,004 ops on the MIDI reader thread — while a fully spec-legal 28-pair recipe repeating one id still pushed a single chip past `asid_player.MAX_OPS_PER_CHIP`, which `pack_slot` then truncated, deleting the *later* chips' writes from the slot. `0x32`'s `chip_index` is likewise clamped to the range the multi-SID commands can produce, so no consumer is handed an index this decoder would never otherwise emit. Every register/type update carries a `chip_index` (0 for `0x4E`, `cmd - 0x50 + 1` for the multi-SID commands, `data0` for `0x32`) so the scene routes it to the matching SID address. The `0x4E` unpack reconstructs each present register's 8-bit value (7 low bits from `register_data`, 8th from the packed MSB bytes) and maps the ASID register ID → SID offset via `_ASID_REG_TO_OFFSET` (keyed by offset `addr - $D400`, so it doubles as a shadow index). **Double control write:** ASID orders the three voice control registers last (IDs 22-27) so a frame can carry a *second* write to each control reg — the gate-off→gate-on hard-restart trick. The decoder keeps the second (final) value in `regs` and surfaces the first in `control_first` for the scene's two-phase emit.
 
 ### `asid_scene.py` — the scene
- `AsidScene(VoiceScopeRenderer, Scene)`, `WANTS_AUDIO_LOCK = True`, is the sibling of `MidiScene` (see below): same VoiceScopeRenderer visualization, same MIDI-port plumbing, a 25-byte `$D400-$D418` shadow + `SIDEmulator` **per chip**. The difference is the input — MidiScene *synthesizes* SID writes from notes/CCs, AsidScene *relays* the finished register bytes — so all synth knobs (waveform/ADSR/filter/voice allocation) are gone; ASID carries that state. It has **two playback paths**, selected by `asid_buffered_player` (auto/on/off): the default *coalesced* path and the *buffered* ring player (next subsection). In the **coalesced** path the reader thread drains SysEx into the per-chip shadows and flushes a hardware block write at `_FLUSH_INTERVAL_S` (≤60 Hz) so a burst or high-multispeed tune can't outrun the ≈200 writes/s DMA ceiling (high multispeed is frame-decimated — this is the limitation the buffered path removes). Each flush is one `write_regs(<chip base>, *shadow)` block write **per dirty chip**; a within-frame hard restart first emits the `control_first` values as individual `write_memory` calls *before* that chip's block (so the gate-off pulse reaches the chip), passing a `retrigger` mask to `update_registers`. Bitmap-only ⇒ `_validate_asid` reports a `hires` display (PETSCII overlays rejected). Info rows: title = name + SID count + play state + SID type; meta = the host's `0x4F` "now playing" text if any, else a per-voice waveform + `VOL nn` summary of the primary chip. `0x31` PAL/NTSC switches every emulator clock live. Teardown stops the ring player first (if buffered), then silences every mapped SID, restores the SID-address config (below), and restores bank-0 + the **char-mode** `$D018` (`D018_CHAR_DEFAULT` = `$14`, matrix at bank+`$0400`, bitmap bit clear) — like MidiScene and WaveformScene, which write the same constant for the same reason. All three used to write `D018_HIRES_BITMAP` here while the comment claimed the char-mode default: the scope's own value, left pointing at the bitmap layout. Each scene's teardown test asserts the **literal** `"14"`, not the constant: comparing the byte teardown wrote against the constant it wrote it from compares the code to itself, and setting `D018_CHAR_DEFAULT = 0x18` left all three green. `tests/test_voice_scope.py` supplies the independent half by driving a real char-mode engage from another module and comparing the constant against what the VIC is actually left holding. The next scene's mode engage owns `$D011`; this write only puts the matrix pointer back where a char mode expects to find it, which is exactly what `hires.py`'s teardown does when its flicker handler may have moved it.
+ `AsidScene(VoiceScopeRenderer, Scene)`, `WANTS_AUDIO_LOCK = True`, is the sibling of `MidiScene` (see below): same VoiceScopeRenderer visualization, same MIDI-port plumbing, a 25-byte `$D400-$D418` shadow + `SIDEmulator` **per chip**. The difference is the input — MidiScene *synthesizes* SID writes from notes/CCs, AsidScene *relays* the finished register bytes — so all synth knobs (waveform/ADSR/filter/voice allocation) are gone; ASID carries that state. It has **two playback paths**, selected by `asid_buffered_player` (auto/on/off): the default *coalesced* path and the *buffered* ring player (next subsection). In the **coalesced** path the reader thread drains SysEx into the per-chip shadows and flushes a hardware block write at `_FLUSH_INTERVAL_S` (≤60 Hz) so a burst or high-multispeed tune can't outrun the ≈200 writes/s DMA ceiling (high multispeed is frame-decimated — this is the limitation the buffered path removes). Each flush is one `write_regs(<chip base>, *shadow)` block write **per dirty chip**; a within-frame hard restart first emits the `control_first` values as individual `write_memory` calls *before* that chip's block (so the gate-off pulse reaches the chip), passing a `retrigger` mask to `update_registers`. Bitmap-only ⇒ `_validate_asid` reports a `hires` display (PETSCII overlays rejected). Info rows: title = name + SID count + play state + SID type; meta = the host's `0x4F` "now playing" text if any, else a per-voice waveform + `VOL nn` summary of the primary chip. `0x31` PAL/NTSC switches every emulator clock live. Teardown stops the ring player first (if buffered), then silences every mapped SID, restores the SID-address config (below), and restores bank-0 + the **char-mode** `$D018` (`VIC.D018_CHAR_DEFAULT` = `$14`, matrix at bank+`$0400`, bitmap bit clear) — like MidiScene and WaveformScene, which write the same constant for the same reason. All three used to write `D018_HIRES_BITMAP` here while the comment claimed the char-mode default: the scope's own value, left pointing at the bitmap layout. Each scene's teardown test asserts the **literal** `"14"`, not the constant: comparing the byte teardown wrote against the constant it wrote it from compares the code to itself, and setting `D018_CHAR_DEFAULT = 0x18` left all three green. `tests/test_voice_scope.py` supplies the independent half by driving a real char-mode engage from another module and comparing the constant against what the VIC is actually left holding. The next scene's mode engage owns `$D011`; this write only puts the matrix pointer back where a char mode expects to find it, which is exactly what `hires.py`'s teardown does when its flicker handler may have moved it.
 
 **Teardown steps are independent guarantees, not a transaction.** All three SID scenes — `AsidScene`, `MidiScene`, `WaveformScene` — owe the next scene a quiescent machine: kernal IRQ vector and CIA #1 latch, every mapped chip silenced, the SID-address config restored, the char-mode `$D018` put back. Each used to sequence that whole list inside a single `try`, so the first raise silently abandoned every restore after it. In `AsidScene` the starved call was `_restore_config`; worse, the comment directly above argued that the IRQ restore must *not* be delegated to the player's own bookkeeping, and then sequenced it behind the `self._player.stop()` that can raise. `WaveformScene` was the widest case — seven restores whose comment reasons carefully about their *order*, all sharing one `try`, with a `finally` protecting exactly one of them. They now run through `run_teardown_steps` (`c64cast/_teardown.py` — it started out in `scenes/scenes.py` beside its first caller and moved to the package root when the audio sources needed it too), which takes `(label, callable)` pairs, guards each one, and logs `teardown step '<label>' failed; continuing` at ERROR before moving on, so a link that dies during silencing still leaves the next scene a char-mode VIC. Catching `Exception` rather than `BaseException` is deliberate: teardown runs on the shutdown path, and a `KeyboardInterrupt` there must propagate instead of being logged as a failed step and discarded. The char-mode display restore all three duplicated is now one `voice_scope.restore_char_mode_display`. The step *order* carries a guarantee of its own in `AsidScene`: the MIDI port closes before anything restores the machine, because the reader's poll stop is a bounded join that `_pollthread` documents as abandoning a worker blocked on the link — and that worker's loop calls `_retune_if_due` on every pass. A closed port is what stops it reading a further `0x31` and putting CIA #1 back on the wire's rate after the restore has already run.
 
